.TH XENIX 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xenix \- run a SCO Xenix/286 program with a suitable environment
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xenix
.I program
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xenix
is a shell script installed as
.IR /usr/bin/xenix .
It is not an emulator: Xenix/286 binaries run directly under the
SCO Xenix/286 personality.
The script only adjusts the environment and then replaces itself with
.I program
and its arguments via the shell's
.BR exec .
.I program
is a path in the Substrate file system, such as
.IR /perso/xenix286s/usr/bin/word .
.PP
Its main job is choosing
.BR TERM .
Microsoft MSTOOLS programs such as Word 3.0 look the terminal up in
.IR $XENIX_ROOT/usr/lib/MSTOOLS/termdesc
and refuse to start if it is not described there, while Substrate's
console login sets
.BR TERM=linux .
.B xenix
sets
.B TERM
as follows:
.IP 1. 4
If
.B XENIX_TERM
is set and non-empty, its value is used.
.IP 2. 4
Otherwise, if
.B TERM
consists only of letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- ,
the termdesc file is readable, and it has an entry beginning with
.IB TERM |
at the start of a line,
.B TERM
is kept.
.IP 3. 4
Otherwise
.B TERM
is set to
.BR vt100 .
.PP
It also creates the directory
.I $XENIX_ROOT/usr/tmp
if it is missing, since MSTOOLS programs write scratch files there
without creating it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
If
.I program
is executed, the exit status is that of
.IR program .
With no arguments,
.B xenix
prints a usage message and exits with status
.BR 64 .
If
.I program
cannot be executed, the status is the one reported by
.BR sh (1)
for a failed
.BR exec .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XENIX_ROOT
Root of the Xenix/286 file tree; default
.IR /perso/xenix286s .
.TP
.B XENIX_TERM
If set, used as
.B TERM
unconditionally.
.TP
.B TERM
Kept if the termdesc file describes it, otherwise replaced as above.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /perso/xenix286s/usr/lib/MSTOOLS/termdesc
MSTOOLS terminal descriptions (under
.BR XENIX_ROOT ).
.TP
.I /perso/xenix286s/usr/tmp
Scratch directory created on demand.
.SH NOTES
The
.B ansi
entry of the termdesc file shipped with MSTOOLS is damaged and makes
Word fail with
.BR "Insufficient memory" .
The source directory
.I bin/xenix
contains
.BR fix\-termdesc\-ansi.sh ,
which replaces that entry with the corrected one in
.BR termdesc\-ansi.entry ;
it is a maintenance script for the Xenix disk image and is not
installed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
$ xenix /perso/xenix286s/usr/bin/word letter.doc
$ XENIX_TERM=wyse50 xenix /perso/xenix286s/usr/bin/word
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR sco_x286 (4),
.BR xout286 (4)
